{"name":"Antiquarian Gold","hex":"#ba8a45","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"antiquarian","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Pertaining to antiquaries, or to antiquity."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A collector or student of, or expert in, antiquities or antiques."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/antiquarian"},{"term":"gold","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A heavy yellow elemental metal of great value, with atomic number 79.","A coin or coinage made of this material, or supposedly so."]},{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["☉ (alchemy)"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gold"}],"entries":[{"title":"Antiquarian","blocks":[{"text":"An antiquarian or antiquary is an aficionado or student of antiquities or things of the past. More specifically, the term is used for those who study history with particular attention to ancient artefacts, archaeological and historic sites, or historic archives and manuscripts. The essence of antiquarianism is a focus on the empirical evidence of the past, and is encapsulated in the motto adopted by the 18th-century antiquary Sir Richard Colt Hoare, \"We speak from facts, not theory.\""}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/1655_-_Frontispiece_of_Museum_Wormiani_Historia.jpg/330px-1655_-_Frontispiece_of_Museum_Wormiani_Historia.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antiquarian","wikidata":"Q5697103"},{"title":"Gold","blocks":[{"text":"Gold is a chemical element; its chemical symbol is Au and atomic number 79. In its pure form, it is a bright-metallic-yellow, dense, soft, malleable, and ductile metal. Chemically, gold is a transition metal, a group 11 element, and one of the noble metals. It is one of the least reactive chemical elements, being the second lowest in the reactivity series, with only platinum ranked as less reactive. Gold is solid under standard conditions."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Gold-crystals.jpg/330px-Gold-crystals.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold","wikidata":"Q897"},{"title":"Gold (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Gold, also called golden, is a color tone resembling the chemical element gold."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg/330px-GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold_(color)","wikidata":"Q208045"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Antiquaries%3B_twenty_portraits_of_historians._Engraving_by_J._Wellcome_V0006811.jpg/500px-Antiquaries%3B_twenty_portraits_of_historians._Engraving_by_J._Wellcome_V0006811.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Antiquaries%3B_twenty_portraits_of_historians._Engraving_by_J._Wellcome_V0006811.jpg/1280px-Antiquaries%3B_twenty_portraits_of_historians._Engraving_by_J._Wellcome_V0006811.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Antiquaries%3B_twenty_portraits_of_historians._Engraving_by_J._Wellcome_V0006811.jpg/500px-Antiquaries%3B_twenty_portraits_of_historians._Engraving_by_J._Wellcome_V0006811.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"\"Antiquaries\": portraits of 20 influential antiquaries and historians published in Crabb's Universal Historical Dictionary (1825). Featured are: Giraldus Cambrensis, John Leland, Guido Panciroli, John Stow, William Camden, Justus Lipsius, Joseph Justus Scaliger, Johannes Meursius, Hubert Goltzius, Henry Spelman, Charles Patin, Philipp Clüver, William Dugdale, Claudius Salmasius, Friedrich Spanheim, Joh